  "art_style": "Raw photorealistic amateur smartphone footage, one unbroken handheld take, vertical 9:16, shot inside a train travelling at extreme speed. Crooked off-centre framing, constant handheld shake, rolling-shutter skew, autofocus hunting, glare, shadow noise, low-bitrate compression and smeared motion blur are all present, but these artefacts NEVER hide the world outside and never change the scene. THE CARRIAGE IS A GLASS OBSERVATION CAR: the left wall is one continuous pane of glass that curves upward and carries on over the passengers into a glass roof, with slim dark ribs arching overhead every few metres. The glass fills at least three quarters of the frame and continues above head height, so the sky and anything tall are visible without the camera moving. The camera looks out through it at about 90 degrees to the direction of travel, tilted slightly up so the roof glass stays in frame. Never look forward or back along the aisle, never leave the carriage, never show the train from outside, no aerial view, no third-person view, no zoom, no cinematic camera move, no stabilization. PASSENGERS RIDE INSIDE AND YOU SEE THEM REACT: between the camera and the glass, along the lower and right edges and much closer to the lens than the window, ride a few ordinary commuters. Light from outside catches them only at the edges: rim-lit outlines and body motion are legible, while faces and interior surfaces stay underexposed. Facial features and identity are never discernible at any distance. The same passengers keep their positions and their clothing for the whole journey, so the ride reads as one continuous take. They cover at most the lowest third of the frame. Beyond the glass there is only the world: no person, no body and no human reflection ever appears on the far side of the glass or mirrored in it. THE PASSENGERS ARE THE SEISMOGRAPH: every jolt is read on their bodies. On the hard hits they are thrown sideways, clutch the poles, duck, recoil, gasp or scream, and one lifts a phone to film; between hits they slump back into tired commuter stillness, which makes the violence read harder. SPEED: the train is always moving forward and never stops, never slows and never arrives. The glass, the poles and the whole frame carry constant high-frequency vibration. Anything within ten metres of the glass crosses the entire frame in under a fifth of a second and is an unreadable streak; mid-distance structures sweep past in well under a second; only the far horizon drifts. THE VIEW CHANGES EVERY TWO OR THREE SECONDS: the journey is cut inside the take by covering events, never by editing. When the glass clears it looks onto a DIFFERENT stretch of the same destination, further along the same line: nearer, deeper, stranger, bigger. THE WORLD ITSELF DOES THE COVERING: the thing that covers the glass is the destination's own medium, a wall of dust, a sheet of spray, a burst of steam, a curtain of ash, a wave of water, driving snow, a swarm, a bank of cloud, or the body of something enormous passing an arm's length away. It slams into the pane, covers it completely and is gone. EVERY RESET THAT SETS THE PACE IS ENVIRONMENTAL; at most ONE structural cover, a tunnel mouth, a bridge pier or a passing train, may appear in a whole journey, and it does not count toward the pace. THE COVER IS BRIEF AND TOTAL: the glass is fully covered for between one eighth and one quarter of a second, never for a whole second, and the train never dwells inside a tunnel, behind a wall or in the dark. The scene may reset ONLY during that moment of complete opacity, when no landmark, no horizon and no trace of the old view remains visible; a translucent or partial cover never permits a reset. Nothing outside ever morphs, dissolves or teleports on screen, and the cover hides only a short stretch of the same route. EVERY COVER IS AN IMPACT: the wipe arrives with a physical blow. The carriage judders and lurches, the camera shakes hard, the poles rattle, the glass booms, and the passengers are thrown and cry out. NO DEAD AIR: between two covers something always enters at the leading edge of the glass, grows as it crosses and exits the trailing edge with violent parallax, or a large event fires. The approaching cover satisfies this by itself: it appears in the distance, swells across the glass and does NOT exit, it fills the frame and becomes the cover. There is never a held wide shot and never a view that simply sits there. SEAM RULE: every shot after the first opens INSIDE the previous view, mid-shake, and its first total cover arrives within the first second and a half. The times written in this shot's own opening lines are binding and nothing later may override them. The LAST SECOND of every shot leaves the glass clear on a readable frame. TRANSIT PHYSICS: the route is physically real and its direction is felt in the carriage, in the pitch of the line, the lean of the bodies, the pressure, the temperature and the side the light comes from. This shot's own text states which way the journey goes; follow it exactly and invent no other kind of travel. CONTRAST CLIMBS AND NEVER WASHES OUT: past the threshold the sky is the DARKEST region of the landscape, every light outside comes from below or from the landmarks themselves, and the carriage interior is darker still. Each shot is darker overhead and hotter in its light source than the one before it. Pale overcast, white haze, flat daylight and low-contrast distance are forbidden past the threshold. NO TEXT ANYWHERE: no signage, no destination board, no letters, no numbers, no subtitles, no captions. Diegetic audio only, never music: hum, carriage rumble, rattling poles, vibrating glass, exterior sound muffled through the closed pane, and the passengers themselves, breath, startled gasps and short screams on the hard impacts. No gore, no graphic injury, no real religious figures, no trademarked property.",
